course: dictionary
lesson: 10701
title: Unsolved: when nobody knows yet
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unsolved is made of three parts: un- means not, solve means to work out the answer, and -ed shows it describes something. So unsolved means not yet worked out. The opposite is solved.
explain: Read the clues in the sentence: «Nobody can climb inside another person's head, so whether the red you see feels exactly like the red I see is a real, unsolved puzzle.» Nobody can check the answer, so the puzzle is still open. That is exactly what unsolved means here.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it in. If you read «a real, not yet answered puzzle» and it still makes sense, your meaning is right. Remember that unsolved is an adjective: it describes a noun such as a mystery, a problem, or a crime.
fact: Unsolved means not yet solved, answered or explained.
fact: The word parts are un- (not), solve (to work out) and -ed.
fact: You can say «an unsolved mystery», «an unsolved crime» or «an unsolved problem».
fact: In the news and in detective stories, an unsolved crime is often called a cold case.
fact: Some famous maths puzzles are still unsolved, and scientists keep working on them.
exercise: «Whether the red you see feels exactly like the red I see is a real, unsolved puzzle.» What does unsolved mean here? | Too hard to see | Solved in the end | *Not yet answered | Painted red
exercise: A detective story is about an unsolved crime. What does unsolved tell you about the crime? | It was a crime about colours | It was solved in the first chapter | *Nobody has found out who did it yet | It happened very quickly
exercise: Which part of unsolved means not? | puzzle | -ed | *un- | solve
test: Mia says her question about why the sky is blue is still unsolved. What does she mean? | She has solved it and forgotten the answer | The sky is not blue | The answer is already written in every book | *Nobody has explained it yet
test: Which phrase would you find in a news story about a crime that police have not yet explained? | a happy ending | *an unsolved crime | a solved mystery | a busy street
exam: In a science article, «Scientists say the cause of the illness remains unsolved», what does remains unsolved mean? | The illness has been cured | *The cause is still not explained | The cause was found last year | The scientists have gone home
